<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Syria Palaestina</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 135–c. 390</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> Palaestina was the Roman province formed when the emperor Hadrian renamed the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> after crushing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Bar Kokhba</a> revolt in 135 CE. The new name, taken from the old Greek term for the coastal land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philistines">Philistines</a>, removed Judaea from the map, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were barred from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, rebuilt as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelia-capitolina">Aelia Capitolina</a>. Around 390 the province was divided into several Palaestina provinces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/syria-palaestina.jpg" alt="Syria Palaestina" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tomisti · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Syria_Palaestina_(AD_210).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> had been under direct Roman rule since the deposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-archelaus">Herod Archelaus</a> in 6 CE, and its capital had shifted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> to Caesarea Maritima. In the 1st and 2nd centuries it became the centre of a series of unsuccessful Jewish rebellions against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The First Jewish-Roman War of 66-73 ended with the destruction of Jerusalem and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Bar Kokhba</a> revolt of 132-136 devastated the countryside of Judea, with many people killed, displaced or sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. The renamed province included Judea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idumaea">Idumaea</a> and Philistia, kept Caesarea Maritima as its capital and stayed distinct from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, whose capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The new name was introduced after the revolt, and the exact date and motives are uncertain. Coins of Hadrian before the revolt still say <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a>, and a military diploma of 139 already uses the new name. Most scholars see a deliberate attempt to suppress Jewish ties to the land, and the historian Werner Eck argues that Judaea alone lost its ethnic name as a punishment, unlike rebellious provinces such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and Britannia. David Jacobson suggests a practical reason, since the province had grown larger than Judea.</p><p>The name Syria Palaestina was already used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-roman-world">Greco-Roman world</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> speaks of that part of Syria called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. Jerusalem was rebuilt as the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelia-capitolina">Aelia Capitolina</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were forbidden to settle there or nearby.</p><p>The province was governed by a consular rank governor, higher than the legate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> had been, and a second legion was stationed at Legio. Around 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> added much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia-petraea">Arabia Petraea</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negev">Negev</a> and Sinai, and moved Legio X Fretensis from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelia-capitolina">Aelia Capitolina</a> to Aila.</p><p>The fourth century brought an economic upswing as the empire became Christian and pilgrimage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> grew. According to the historian Moshe David Herr, the Jewish share of the population fell after the revolt from about two thirds to three fifths or less, and by the end of the 4th century to about one third. Christians formed a majority by the fifth century. The province was split into smaller ones in the 4th and 5th centuries, among them Palaestina Prima, Palaestina Secunda and Palaestina Salutaris, within the Diocese of the East.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Syria Palaestina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In antiquity there were two major centres of such scholarship, the academies of <b>Syria Palaestina</b> and of Babylonia, and a Talmud was compiled in each.</blockquote><a href="/work/talmud">Talmud · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Judea was devastated, renamed <b>Syria Palaestina</b>, and Jews were barred from Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/state/bar-kokhba-state">Bar Kokhba state</a></li></ul></section>
